A||N||7||CJ||88 GT Oil Leak||||8||
Z||000000||CJ||10-06-2001||09:49 AM||||We have a slight engine oil leak, perhaps a drop or so on the driveway overnight after driving the car.  The bottom and parts of the side of the oil pan are wet with oil.  The bottom of the pan is quite rusty.  Doesn't seem to be coming from the rocker cover or anywhere high up on the engine.<P>I had the car in my garage for the last five days, on jackstands.  The left side of the vehicle was somewhat higher than the right side, about 4-6 inches perhaps.<P>After being in this position for 5 days, there was a puddle of oil on the floor, about 12 inches in diameter.<P>I wiped the pan, tried to see where the oil is coming from, and couldn't really tell.  The oil level was ok, may have been slightly overfilled prior to this leak.<P>I put the car back on the level garage floor, and parked it overnight.  This morning, there are no drops on the floor.<P>My theory is that either the oil pan gasket at the front of the engine (i.e. right side of the vehicle) is leaking.  When the vehicle is level, the oil is not at that level in the pan.  Some gets spashed up there, resulting in the few drops we get on the floor.  When the car was on stands, with that side of the car lower, the oil was above that point, and poured out over the course of five days.<P>Any thoughts on my theory or other experiences with oil leaks on this?||||reg||8
Z||000001||SKIDMARK||10-06-2001||10:01 AM||||Sounds reasonable but the front crankshaft seal will do the same thing. Check for oil on the belt and back side of the pully. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||10-06-2001||12:20 PM||||BEFORE you do that, replace the o-ring on the distributer. It may not be the cause in this case but it has in many, many other cases. The oil just makes its way down the block in so many different ways to fool people into thinking its something else.<P>Before getting expensive, try the easy answers first.The worst that can happen is that you still have an oil leak. <P>Let us know what you find out!||||reg||6
Z||000003||Pike||10-06-2001||11:31 PM||||Sounds like an oil pressure leak to me. If you wipe everything off and you get no leaks...you probably will after running the car again. Check the oil pressure sensor pick up tube. It could be cracked...||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||10-06-2001||11:39 PM||||The Balancer gets a groove worn in it by the timing chain cover seal.  A balancer sleeve from Fel-pro and a new seal will cost you about $25 together.  Simple job.  Oil from here is also a common cause of belt squeal, too, along with antifreeze (also oily) from the water pump weep hole.<BR>The distributor O-ring gets hardened over  time, and won't seal any more.  Easy fix, also.<BR>I've had to fix both of these on mine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||10-06-2001||11:40 PM||||Take a close look at the oil pan itself too. I was very surprised to find pinholes in the oil pan on my '88, particularly considering that the rest of the car is clean and rust free and only has bout 50K miles on it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||pHoOl||10-07-2001||03:07 AM||||Sounds like maybe we should see if that guy will let us borrow his garage and do the oil pan swap.<P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>        <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||CJ||10-08-2001||10:59 AM||||I ended up giving up on the idea of doing the brake job myself, and took it in to the local shop.  Also had them investigate the oil leak.<P>Turns out my original suspicion was correct, the oil pan is rusted out!<P>Continuing to take the easy way out, I'll have them install the oil pan and gasket I bought a while ago...<P>Thanks for all the feedback...<P>||||reg||10
